## Lemma

Suppose the already exported matrix body contains exactly the rows indexed by
`S_50000`, and independently exported complement chunks contain exactly the
rows indexed by `S_comp`.  Number the reused rows `1,...,50000` and the
complement rows `50001,...,92979`, retaining all 39,726 target columns.  Then
body concatenation emits a row permutation of the full selected Macaulay map.
Consequently it has exactly the same row span, rank, minors and cokernel as the
natural-order full map.

## Proof

The recomputed source lists are finite lists of distinct pairs consisting of a
selected derivative-generator identifier and a certified standard multiplier
exponent.  Set equality and cardinality prove

\[
S_{50000}\cap S_{\rm comp}=\varnothing,
\qquad
S_{50000}\cup S_{\rm comp}=S_{\rm all}.
\]

Every exported matrix row is the exact reduction of its indexed relation
multiple into the common target-coordinate basis.  Concatenation changes only
the order assigned to the source rows.  Thus the concatenated matrix is
`P M_all` for a permutation matrix `P`.  Since `P` is invertible, left
multiplication by `P` preserves row span and rank.  It also induces a bijection
between nonzero row minors, while the target and cokernel are unchanged.

No zero padding, fitted row, inferred coefficient or observed value is added.

